[Music]  okay this is a lightning talk called  code or die we're at PI data Warsaw it's  Friday October 10th to 2017 I'm James  Powell if you like this talk you can  follow me on twitter at don't use this  code this is a talk about a hobby  project that I've been working on over  the past few weeks and as many of you  know hobby projects are a big  distraction in fact one of the best  things about hobby projects is that they  can be very distracting so before I tell  you about coder die I want to tell you  about a couple of things that distracted  me from doing the hobby project that has  been distracting me from doing actual  real work the first one is a library  that I have written called PG SQL parse  most of the things that I'll show you in  this talk are about 50 to 70 percent  done so just wait a little bit before  they're ready to use PG SQL parse is  built on a library called Lib PG Curie  which is an extraction of the query  engine the query the parser from the  from Postgres itself and so what I can  do in Python is I can take string SQL I  can parse it into a Python ast or rather  into a Python representation of the SQL  ast and I do neat things like I can look  at the where clause I can take another  piece of SQL and I can merge them so in  this block of code here I'm taking this  query and I'm sticking it under this  common table expression which is kind of  neat to be able to do in a systematic  fashion now that doesn't seem very  useful so the reason that this was the  lowest level of the Hobby project is  because I needed this in order to do  another hobby project called gormless  and it's kind of a weird name I'll  explain the name to really quickly I  wanted to create a norm for use with  Postgres but I hate ORMs so I wanted to  create like an anti orb and that's low  you could call like PG or malice or in  other words gormless an english gormless  means kind of stupid and this is  essentially a pretty stupid idea what I  have is I have a way to map arbitrary  SQL to the Python object model to Python  methods and python descriptors so here  you can see some base object you can  look up you can look it up by ID by name  and do inserts and deletes here you have  some object it represents a ship you'll  fit you'll see why I call this ship  later and it has some fields like a  speed location there are some looks all  kind of like a uglier version of a  normal  where I have some foreign keys I have  some normal fields that are just  properties and I have some other methods  well the neat thing I can do is I can do  this I can select from the database and  get a Python object out so this thing  ship here is not a tuple it's not  anything else it's an actual raw Python  object and when I have a Python object I  can also do things like ship top by ID  and and pull it out by ID instead of  using a raw query but once I have a  Python object can do things like ship  that location and will give me another  Python object representing the location  of this ship and whatever that might  mean once I can do that I can do neat  things I can say ship location dot  nearby ships to find all the ships that  happen to be nearby that one where this  is a Python property this is a Python  property and it corresponds to a couple  of SQL lookups against my database  itself the interesting thing is I can  then arbitrarily add adapters on top of  that so here ship dot all names is all  the different names that might refer to  this ship notice this is just a property  lookup this gives you a Python  dictionary that has its insert and  removal methods over overwritten so when  I do something like this I do a set item  on the dictionary it actually goes and  updates the database when I do a Dell  item on the dictionary that also  corresponds to a database operation most  ORM s don't give you any of this it only  give you mechanism to interact with the  database via functions not via arbitrary  Python syntax this is an actual Python  dictionary that whenever you perform an  operation on it it transparently updates  a database behind the scenes one neat  thing I can do is if you look closely  here there was this little thing called  notifications well I can start a  separate thread so that if another  process completely separate from mine  updates the object in the database I get  a notification in my Python process and  it transparently updates the database so  this ship object here is always  up-to-date with the current state of the  database or whatever notifications I've  received so I can have multiple scripts  run against this database and I never  have to worry about reloading something  because they're being transparently  reloaded by a thread in the background  that's kind of cool I can also do  something really neat which is I can set  context on my cursor so I can create a  common table expression like this and  then that applies itself using my ast  parser against every operation that  occurs under that context manager so  this ship thought locations out nearby  ships not home world nearby ships is a  bunch of SQL look  each of which is now being wrapped in  this common table expression that's why  it was useful to have that tool so even  this update operation that looks like  just a Ross string is actually being  wrapped in that common table expression  which is very useful for now doing very  complex very very complex lookups  against the database and transforming  them into single SQL statements very  efficient ones I can also create  arbitrary queries so here's the  definition for a query I select some  from and some - from some database here  the parameters I pass it I give it a  graph adapter so that I can now select  things out of my database and turn them  into Network X graphs most ORM s give  you one table row equals one object my  RM is not really an ORM it's just an  arbitrary mapping of SQL to Python the  Python data model so I can take subsets  of tables and turn them into Network X  graphs no other RM can do anything close  to that similarly if I did something  like scores here I can give it an array  adapter and I can select subsets of my  database of subsets of rows in my  database and transparently stick it into  an umpire in the array and when you  update that number numpy in the array it  transparently updates the database  contents itself nobody else can do that  why because who cares right well I came  up with an idea for a game many of you  have played the game risk it looks kind  of like this and I thought what if we  took the game risk and we set it in  space we added spaceship solar systems  neat things like that because we don't  want to be sued for copyright  infringement we make it a real-time game  we take away the game board in the game  pieces entirely and we replaced them  with a JSON API so using a tool HTTP I  could give any arbitrary semi-technical  user this command and they could kind of  figure out what it does  it's an HTTP request I get all the  systems you can probably kind of guess  what that does even if you don't know a  lot about JSON API even if you don't  know a lot about the command line or  about Python you can guess what this  does it gets all the ships you could  guess what this probably does it gets  the system number 32 you could probably  guess what this does it puts an order on  ship number 12 and tells it to move to  some destination it's a very simple very  low-level JSON API what I could do is I  could give players of this game a  will hand out with curl commands and  they could play the game by just  manually writing those curl commands in  the console anyone could play this game  but and what they do is they start with  something as simple like as this that I  wrote for them and they'd start kind of  playing around with the parameters  they'd say Oh  12 to 23 or that moves ship 23 not ship  12 going from this this moves it from  Mars to Venus well interestingly enough  for the first 30 minutes of the game  people could move about 10 ships around  which is manual API calls it would be  really tedious but what if I'm scaled up  the game to a hundred ships then you'd  have to write a bash script so the  newbie will have to come to me and say  well how do i do these commands in a  loop because i can't type these fast  enough to compete in the game and you  tell them well this is how you write a  simple bash script and as they write  more and more complex bash scripts  they'll say this sucks so what are they  gonna do they're gonna write Python  scripts the API output is JSON that  looks something like this you're gonna  get bug eyed if you look at this too  closely and the newbies gonna say I  cannot interpret these results quickly  enough to play the game and you're gonna  tell them well why don't you write a  report using Python here I'll tell you  two about F strings I'll teach you about  the request library I'll teach you about  all the things you need to be  competitive in the game maybe you could  even write a web front-end so I'll teach  you about Django and flask and that  might help you play the game and win  against your friends what happens when  there's hundreds of ships moving in real  time even if you had scripts and a JSON  API even if you had a meta API and  scripts and things like that you won't  be able to compete so what do you have  to do you have to write a bot you have  to use simple AI techniques in order to  compete in the game I like this game  because you start very simple and you  can get arbitrarily complex driven by  the desire of the players to be  competitive at the game even newbies can  play by just copying pasting commands so  their terminal but they will through  natural motivation of wanting to be  competitive in this game come to you and  say how do I get better teach me more  about programming I need to know how to  do this tie this explicit tangible goal  that I have in playing the game to some  arbitrary abstract piece of programming  knowledge it is a very strong motivation  to learn programming and to improve your  programming because the worse your code  is the worse your performance in the  game will be I like this also because  it's an approach to programming via  automation and the in the automation  view perfect  doesn't matter the only thing that  matters in this game it's not perfect  code it's not writing the best code it's  about competing  it's about galactic domination so that's  code or die it's the rules are on github  the other two libraries will be posted  to github but once they're a little bit  closer to complete  I hope to talk about this game more next  year when it's fully developed the API  is going to be very simple and the whole  goal is how do we take somebody who  wants the program but who finds most  materials for programming too simple and  too sorry not too simple but too  abstract too detached from the actual  test they want to do and to make the  motivations very clear very obvious and  to drive the learning from the  perspective of the learner trying to do  something very specific like how do I  compete how do I beat my friend how do I  move my ships fast enough how do I do  this in a loop I really like this idea  it's something I'll be working on more  in 2018 I hope some of you might be  interested in contributing thank you  [Applause]

